NODES += [
 ("Operation", None, 1, None, None, [
  (W, "Operation is the second large part of the chapter, and it is about what happens after the software has been built or downloaded: how a node is started, how it is configured, how its state is observed, and what kind of wallet comes with it. The book introduces it with the statement that the peer-to-peer network is composed of network nodes, run mostly by individuals and some of the businesses that provide Bitcoin services, and that those who run them have a direct and authoritative view of the blockchain, with a local copy of all the spendable bitcoins independently validated by their own system [AH]. A branch about operation therefore describes the daily practice of that independence."),
  (Y, "The branch matters because a node that is badly run gives little of what it promises. A node that is not given enough disk space cannot store the chain, a node whose cache is too small spends its time reading the disk, and a node whose settings nobody understands may expose services that were meant to stay private. The book lists the reasons for running a node in a short list: not relying on any third party to validate the transactions you receive, not disclosing to third parties which transactions belong to your wallet, having programmable access to the network and the blockchain, validating transactions according to the consensus rules in an application, and supporting the network [AH]. Each of these reasons is only as good as the way the node is operated."),
  (E, "A reader meets operation in the configuration file that sits in the data directory, in the messages that the program prints when it starts, and in the answers of the status commands that the chapter calls just after starting the node. The evidence collected for this version comes from a real run of the release 31.1 as an offline node, whose start-up messages, data directory and answers are quoted in the concepts of this branch [BC]. The book's own example is a node that holds the whole chain, keeps an index of all transactions on a very large disk and sends an e-mail when an alert is raised, together with a second example for a small server."),
  (H, "The branch has three groups. The configuration group explains the file and the options that shape the node: where the file and the data directory are found, how the database cache and the mempool limit divide the memory, what the transaction index is for, what blocks-only mode saves and how the alert command works. The running group explains how the node is started in the background, what it writes to its log when it starts, and how two status commands show where it stands in the chain and in the network. The wallet group explains the wallet that Bitcoin Core carries, the older kind that can no longer be created, and the descriptions of scripts on which the newer kind is built."),
  (K, "Two cautions apply to the whole branch. The first is that many of the book's figures belong to the release it printed: the default cache size, the wallet database and the first lines of the log differ in the current release, and the concepts of this branch give both values. The second is the warning in Bitcoin Core's own documentation, which tells users never to make configuration changes that they do not understand and to be wary of accepting any configuration changes provided to them by another source, even if they believe that they do understand them [BC]. A copied configuration file is a set of instructions to a program that holds money-related data."),
 ], None),
 ("Configuration", None, 2, "Operation", None, [
  (W, "Configuration is the group of concepts that describe how a user tells Bitcoin Core how to behave. The book says that the node looks for a configuration file in its data directory on every start, and that the program offers more than a hundred options that modify the behavior of the network node, the storage of the blockchain and many other aspects of its operation [AH]. The group covers the file itself, the idea of an option and the order in which its sources are read, the directory that holds the data, and the options the book singles out as the most important."),
  (Y, "A node that runs on a default setting is usable, but the options are how it is adapted to the machine and to the purpose. The book's two sample files show the range: one is for a node that serves as the back end of a Bitcoin application and puts its data on a disk with a lot of room, the other is for a resource-constrained server and limits the cache, the mempool, the stored blocks and the traffic [AH]. Knowing what each option does is what turns a copied example into a deliberate decision."),
  (E, "The options meet the reader in three forms: as lines of the configuration file, as parameters after the name of the program on the command line, and as entries in the help text, which the version 31.1 prints with 139 option entries, more than the hundred of the book [BC]. The start-up messages of the node list the settings that it has actually read, so the log is the place where an option can be checked. The concepts that follow take the file, the option, the directory and then the individual settings."),
  (H, "The order of the concepts follows the order of the book. The file and its options come first because everything else is set there, followed by the data directory, the database cache, the transaction index, blocks-only mode, the mempool limit and the alert command. Four of them concern memory and disk, which are the resources the node consumes, one concerns bandwidth and one concerns a message to the owner. Each concept gives the book's value and the value that the current release shows."),
 ], None),
 ("ConfigurationFile", "Configuration file", 3, "Configuration", ("bitcoin.conf in the data directory", "Bitcoin Core reads bitcoin.conf from its data directory at every start; options can also be given on the command line, and bitcoind -printtoconsole shows where the file is looked for.", (_CONF_SAMPLE, "{'datadir': '/lotsofspace/bitcoin', 'txindex': '1'}")), [
  (W, "The configuration file is a plain text file, named bitcoin.conf by default, in which the owner of a node writes the options that the program should use. Bitcoin Core's documentation describes its format: it consists of option=value entries, one per line, with the whitespace around them removed, an option is written without the leading hyphen that the command line needs, and a value is mandatory [BC]. The book says that Bitcoin Core will look for a configuration file in its data directory on every start [AH]."),
  (Y, "A file is the practical way to keep a node's settings, because a node is meant to run for months and to restart after every power failure or update. The same options could be typed after the program name every time, but a file records them in one place that can be read, copied, compared and kept under control. It also keeps the settings out of the way of the start-up scripts that the book mentions in the end of the section, which only have to start the program [AH]."),
  (E, "The book shows how to find the file: run bitcoind -printtoconsole in a terminal and look at the first lines of the output, one of which begins with Config file followed by the path [AH]. The log of the 31.1 release in the evidence shows the same line, together with one line for each setting that was read from the file, which is how the settings of that offline node (no connections, no seed lookup) were recorded [BC]. The documentation lists the default places for the three common systems and says that the file is not created by the software and must be created by the user [BC]."),
  (H, "The parser is simple. Blank lines are ignored, a comment starts with a number sign and runs to the end of the line, and a block with a name in square brackets, such as the one named main, holds options for one network only, which take precedence over options without a section [BC]. An option can be negated with the prefix no, so that nofoo=1 turns off an option called foo, and a second file can be added with the option includeconf [BC]. A small worked example splits the lines of a file at the first equals sign and gives a dictionary of option names and values, as the example shows for the data directory and the transaction index of the book's first sample."),
  (K, "Changes to the file take effect only after the node has been restarted, as the documentation states, so editing the file of a running node changes nothing until then [BC]. The file is not a command language, and an option that the program does not know is not an error but shows up as a warning in the log, so a mistyped name can go unnoticed if nobody reads it. A last caution is that the file may hold secrets, for example the hashed credential of the interface, and should be readable only by the user who runs the node, which the init documentation also recommends for the file and the data directory [BC]."),
 ]),
 ("ConfigurationOption", "Configuration option", 3, "Configuration", ("dbcache on the command line and in the file", "An option has a name and a value and can come from the file, from the dynamic settings file or from the command line; the command line wins.", ("(lambda conf, cli: {**conf, **cli})({'dbcache': '450', 'txindex': '1'}, {'dbcache': '2048'})", "{'dbcache': '2048', 'txindex': '1'}")), [
  (W, "A configuration option is a named setting of the program, written in the book and in the help text with a leading hyphen, such as the setting that sets the size of the database cache. Most options take a value, some are switches that are off unless they are set, and almost all of them can be negated with the prefix no, which in general sets a boolean or numeric option to zero and a text option to an empty string, with some exceptions that the documentation names [BC]. The book lists eight of them as the most important and says that Bitcoin Core offers more than 100 [AH]."),
  (Y, "The options are the program's interface for its owner, and the order in which their sources are read decides which value is used. The documentation states that all command-line options except the help, version and conf options may be specified in the configuration file, that all file options except the one that includes another file may also be given on the command line, and that command-line options override values set in the file [BC]. A script that starts the node can therefore change one value for one run without editing the file."),
  (E, "A reader meets the options in the output of the help command of the program, which prints each option with its meaning and its default, such as the line for the database cache that gives a minimum of 4 and a default of 1024 in the current release [BC]. They are also met in the log, where the start-up lines show whether a value came from the file or from the command line, and in the file named settings.json in the data directory, which holds values set at run time through the graphical client or the interface, and which the documentation ranks between the file and the command line [BC]."),
  (H, "The precedence can be shown with two dictionaries. The file says 450 for the cache and 1 for the transaction index; the command line says 2048 for the cache. Merging the file's dictionary with the command line's, the second last, gives the cache of 2048 and keeps the index, which is what the example computes. Network-specific options add a second rule: an option in a section for one network, or prefixed with the network's name, takes precedence over the same option without it [BC]."),
  (K, "The help text and the book must not be trusted blindly for the defaults, because defaults change from release to release: the book's value for the database cache is 450 and the current one is 1024 for machines with enough memory, and the minimum relay fee dropped in release 30.0 [AH] [BC]. Another common mistake is to put an option that is valid only on the command line, such as the one that names the file itself, into the file that it names, where it cannot work. A third is to forget that negation exists: a line written with the prefix no switches something off and is not a typing error."),
 ]),
 ("DataDirectory", "Data directory", 3, "Configuration", ("the .bitcoin folder", "The data directory is where Bitcoin Core keeps the blocks, the unspent-output database, the logs and the wallets; its default place depends on the system and its contents on the network.", ("{'main': '', 'test': 'testnet3/', 'testnet4': 'testnet4/', 'signet': 'signet/', 'regtest': 'regtest/'}['regtest']", "'regtest/'")), [
  (W, "The data directory is the folder in which Bitcoin Core stores everything it has to remember between runs. The documentation describes it as the default location where the files of the program are stored, and gives the default place for each system: a folder named .bitcoin in the user's home directory on Linux, a folder called Bitcoin under Application Support on macOS, and a folder called Bitcoin under the local application data on Windows [BC]. The book says the same for the .bitcoin folder, and that the option datadir selects the directory and filesystem in which to put all the blockchain data [AH]."),
  (Y, "The directory is the node's memory, so where it lives decides whether the node can run at all. The book says that, depending on the configuration, it can use from about 10 GB to almost 1 TB as of the writing, and that the maximum is expected to grow by several hundred gigabytes per year [AH]. For that reason the first sample file puts the directory on a separate disk with plenty of room, and a user who has a small system disk and a large second disk needs this option before the first start."),
  (E, "The evidence for this version shows what a young node keeps there. A freshly started release 31.1, with no peers, had in its directory a file for the banned addresses, the configuration file written for the run, the process identifier file, the folder of blocks, the folder of the unspent-output database called chainstate, the debug log, the folder of the wallet named demo, the fee estimates, the mempool dump, the peer address database and the settings file [BC]. The documentation explains the others in the layout table: a LevelDB database holds the block index, the files blkNNNNN.dat hold the blocks in network format at 128 MiB per file, and the optional index folders appear when an index is switched on [BC]."),
  (H, "All content of the directory except the configuration file is specific to one network. The main network uses the directory itself, and the others use subfolders: testnet3, testnet4, signet and regtest, as the lookup in the worked example shows for regtest [BC]. The program keeps a lock file in the directory, writes its process identifier into a file while it runs and creates the cookie file there too, which is a reason why the access to the directory is a security matter."),
  (K, "A directory is not a place to copy files from one running node to another. The documentation warns that a wallet file must not be shared across different node instances, because that can result in key reuse and double spends, and that a backup of a wallet should be made with the backup call so that the file is locked while it is copied [BC]. It also advises against the exFAT filesystem on macOS, because of reports of database corruption [BC]. The cheapest mistake, however, is to fill the disk: a node that cannot write stops, and that is the reason to plan the space."),
 ]),
 ("DatabaseCache", "Database cache (dbcache)", 3, "Configuration", ("dbcache", "The dbcache option sizes the cache of the unspent-output set: 450 MiB by default in the book, 1024 MiB by default from Bitcoin Core 31.0 on machines with enough memory.", (_CACHE_SPLIT, "(2, 8, 1014)")), [
  (W, "The database cache is a region of memory in which the node keeps part of the set of unspent outputs, so that it does not have to read the disk for every output it needs to check. The option that sizes it is called dbcache, and the book describes it as the size of the UTXO cache, with a default of 450 mebibytes, the advice being to increase it on high-end hardware to read and write the disk less often, or reduce it on low-end hardware to save memory at the expense of using the disk more frequently [AH]. A mebibyte is 1024 times 1024 bytes."),
  (Y, "The cache matters most while the node is catching up with the chain. The documentation on reducing memory says that a lower value makes the initial sync time much longer, and that after the first synchronization the effect is less pronounced for most uses, unless fast validation of blocks is important, such as for mining [BC]. The same page warns that when the operating system starts swapping memory to disk continuously, the node can slow to a crawl, which is why a too-large value is also a mistake."),
  (E, "The current value differs from the book. Release 31.0 raised the default to 1024 MiB from 450 MiB on systems where at least 4096 MiB of RAM is detected, and the release notes say that to keep the previous behavior one sets dbcache to 450 [BC]. The help of the release 31.1 states a minimum of 4 and a default of 1024, and the source shows the rule: the larger value is chosen only on a 64-bit system with the required memory, and the old 450 MiB remains the default in all other cases [BC]."),
  (H, "The configured total is divided before it is used. The source gives the block index database up to 2 MiB, which is the smaller of that value and an eighth of the total, then the database of the chain state up to 8 MiB, which is the smaller of that value and half of what remains, and the rest goes to the cache of unspent outputs [BC]. For 1024 MiB the worked example returns 2, 8 and 1014, which are the three numbers that the start-up log of the evidence prints for the block index, the chain state and the in-memory set of unspent outputs [BC]. If the transaction index is on, ten percent of the total goes to it first."),
  (K, "The memory is shared with the mempool, which the log shows by saying that up to 286.1 MiB of unused mempool space can be added to the 1014 MiB, a figure that the mempool limit concept explains [BC]. Setting the value larger than the memory that is really available is a mistake that containers make easy, because the detected RAM may exceed what the container may use, a case that the release notes mention, and the node may then run out of memory [BC]. The node also logs a warning when it believes a value is too large for the detected memory."),
 ]),
 ("TransactionIndex", "Transaction index (txindex)", 3, "Configuration", ("txindex=1", "The txindex option builds an index of every transaction so that getrawtransaction can find any of them; it cannot be combined with pruning.", ("round(1024 * 10 / 100, 1)", "102.4")), [
  (W, "The transaction index is an optional database that lets the node find any confirmed transaction by its identifier. The book explains that by default Bitcoin Core builds a database containing only the transactions related to the user's wallet, and that to access any transaction with commands like getrawtransaction one has to configure the node to build a complete index with the option txindex set to 1 [AH]. The help of the release 31.1 describes it as a full transaction index used by the getrawtransaction call, off by default [BC]."),
  (Y, "Without the index the node still has the whole chain, but it has no quick way from a transaction identifier to the block that holds it. The question matters to programs that look up arbitrary transactions, such as an explorer or the application back end of the book's first sample file. The book adds that if the option is not set at first and later set, one has to wait for the index to be rebuilt, and that the lookup works provided the block containing the transaction has not been pruned [AH]."),
  (E, "The index is met in the section that follows, where the book decodes Alice's transaction by asking for it by its identifier, and in the data directory, where the documentation says that it lives in a LevelDB database in a folder named indexes/txindex and exists only if the option is on [AH] [BC]. It is also met in the way the cache is split: when the index is enabled the source gives it ten percent of the total cache, up to one gibibyte, before the remainder goes to the set of unspent outputs; for 1024 MiB that is 102.4 MiB, as the example calculates [BC]."),
  (H, "The source of the index describes it as written to a LevelDB database that records the block sequence number and the serialized block offset of each transaction by transaction hash [BC]. A call such as getrawtransaction with an identifier looks the identifier up in the index, reads the block from the disk and returns the transaction. Building the first index means going through all the blocks, which explains the waiting time of the book. The index does not store the transactions again; it only stores where to find them, so its size is far smaller than the blocks themselves."),
  (K, "The index cannot be combined with pruning. The help text of the pruning option says the mode is incompatible with the transaction index, and the run of release 31.1 in the evidence confirms it: starting with both ends with the error that prune mode is incompatible with txindex [BC]. The help of the current release is more exact than the book about what happens without the index: by default the call for a raw transaction returns a transaction only if it is in the mempool, and if the caller names a block, it returns the transaction when the block is available and contains it [BC]. So a node without the index can still answer if it is told in which block to look, but not otherwise. Finally, the index is a convenience for the owner, and it is not part of the consensus rules."),
 ]),
 ("BlocksOnlyMode", "Blocks-only mode", 3, "Configuration", ("blocksonly=1", "In blocks-only mode a node accepts blocks from its peers but not unconfirmed transactions, which saves bandwidth and memory.", ("300 // 5", "60")), [
  (W, "Blocks-only mode is a setting in which the node does not take part in the relay of unconfirmed transactions. The book describes the option as a way to minimize bandwidth usage by only accepting blocks of confirmed transactions from the peers instead of relaying unconfirmed transactions [AH]. The help text of release 31.1 is more exact: it rejects transactions from network peers, switches off the automatic broadcast and rebroadcast of transactions, unless the source peer has the forcerelay permission, and leaves transactions sent through the RPC interface unaffected [BC]."),
  (Y, "The mode exists because relaying every transaction is the largest continuing cost of a node after the first download. A node in this mode still downloads and validates every block, so it enforces the same rules and remains a full node, but it receives each transaction only once, inside a block, and it holds no pool of unconfirmed transactions. The documentation on reducing memory says that the mode reduces the default memory use of the mempool to 5 MB, and that the source defines the blocks-only default as 5, against 300 for a normal node [BC]."),
  (E, "The book uses the option in its second sample file, together with pruning and small caches, for a node on a resource-constrained server [AH]. It is also useful where the owner pays for each gigabyte of upload, and the book recommends against running a node on a metered connection or running it in a way that constrains its bandwidth [AH]. The documentation on reducing memory names it among the ways to use less memory [BC]."),
  (H, "In normal operation a node announces and requests unconfirmed transactions from its peers and keeps them in its mempool, from where it can tell a miner or a wallet about them. In blocks-only mode it skips the request, so the mempool stays nearly empty, and the node learns of a payment only when the payment is confirmed in a block. The worked example divides the normal default of 300 megabytes by the blocks-only default of 5, which shows that the pool of a blocks-only node may be sixty times smaller."),
  (K, "The mode has a privacy cost that the documentation spells out: it must not be used when the node is also used to broadcast transactions, because any transaction sent will stick out like a sore thumb, and when it is combined with the wallet, the wallet should be told not to broadcast and not to spend unconfirmed change, and another mechanism for broadcasting should be used [BC]. Because such a node does not receive transactions that are not yet in a block, a merchant who wants to see a payment before its confirmation cannot rely on it."),
 ]),
 ("MempoolLimit", "Mempool limit (maxmempool)", 3, "Configuration", ("maxmempool=150", "The maxmempool option limits the memory used for unconfirmed transactions; the default is 300 megabytes, and unused mempool memory is lent to the database cache.", ("round(300 * 10**6 / 2**20, 1)", "286.1")), [
  (W, "The mempool limit is the maximum size of the pool of unconfirmed transactions that the node holds, set by the option maxmempool. The book describes it as limiting the transaction memory pool to this many megabytes, and says to use it to reduce memory use on memory-constrained nodes; its second sample file sets it to 150 [AH]. The help text of release 31.1 gives the default as 300 megabytes, and the documentation on reducing memory says the unit is the megabyte of 1000 bytes, not the mebibyte, and that the minimum is 5 [BC]."),
  (Y, "A pool that has no limit would let any sender fill the memory of every node with unconfirmed transactions. The limit forces the node to decide which transactions to keep, and the documentation says that a lower maximum means that transactions will be evicted sooner, which affects any use of the node that processes unconfirmed transactions [BC]. Chapter 2 explained that a transaction waits in the mempool until it is mined; this is the bound on how long and how many can wait."),
  (E, "A reader meets the figure in the start-up log. For the default of 300 megabytes, the log of the release 31.1 in the evidence says that the in-memory set of unspent outputs has 1014.0 MiB, plus up to 286.1 MiB of unused mempool space [BC]. The source explains why: the limit in bytes is the number of megabytes times 1,000,000, and the unused part is lent to the cache of unspent outputs, so that the memory the node uses in total stays near the sum of the two settings [BC]."),
  (H, "The conversion explains the odd-looking figure: 300 megabytes is 300 times 10 to the sixth bytes, and dividing by 2 to the twentieth bytes per mebibyte gives 286.1 mebibytes, as the worked example computes. The mempool and the cache therefore draw on one memory budget, which is why the documentation recommends limiting the mempool when trying to reduce the total memory use, and why the blocks-only mode, which needs only 5 MB, leaves more for the cache [BC]."),
  (K, "The unit is the usual mistake: a number of megabytes of 1000 bytes is smaller than the same number of mebibytes, and the two options that look alike, the mempool limit and the database cache, use different units. A node with a small mempool also forgets low-fee transactions sooner, so a wallet that depends on the node may see its payment dropped from that node's pool while other nodes still hold it. Bitcoin Core also expires transactions after a number of hours, an option that the help lists, so the limit is not the only bound."),
 ]),
 ("AlertNotify", "Alert notification (alertnotify)", 3, "Configuration", ("alertnotify=myemailscript.sh", "The alertnotify option runs a command of the owner's choice when the node raises an alert, replacing %s with the message, which the node first cleans and puts in single quotes.", (_ALERT, "'myemailscript.sh \"Alert: \\'x; reboot; \\'\"'")), [
  (W, "The alert notification is a hook that lets a node tell its owner that something is wrong. The book describes the option as running a specified command or script to send emergency alerts to the owner of the node, and both its sample files set it to a script that sends an e-mail with the text Alert followed by the message [AH]. The help text says that the command is executed when an alert is raised and that the symbol %s in the command is replaced by the message [BC]."),
  (Y, "A node runs without anybody watching it, and some events need a person. Bitcoin Core's source shows two situations in which the node raises such a warning: when it finds an invalid chain with more work than its own best chain by more than six blocks of work, which could be due to database corruption or to consensus incompatibility with the peers, and when blocks signal new rules that this version of the software does not know, with the message that unknown new rules were activated and the number of the version bit [BC]. Both are signs that the owner's software may be out of step with the network, a case that the soft fork concept explains."),
  (E, "The reader meets the option in the book's two sample files and in the help text. In practice it is a line that points to a script, which may send an e-mail, a message to a phone or an entry in a monitoring system. Since the script is the owner's, the node gives no idea of what is done with the message, and it does not wait for it: the source starts the command in a thread that runs free, so a slow script cannot hold up the node [BC]."),
  (H, "When a warning is set, the node calls a function that reads the command from the option, takes the warning text, strips every character that is not a letter, a digit, a blank or one of the characters in the string .,;-_/:?@(), puts single quotes around the result and replaces each %s in the command with it [BC]. The worked example copies these steps for a hostile message that tries to close the quote and add a command: the quote itself is removed, so the text stays inside one quoted word and the final command line is harmless. The rules of the shell for single quotes are the reason the technique works."),
  (K, "The cleaning protects against the message but not against the owner's own script. A script that passes its argument on without quoting it can still be abused, and the %s must be inside the quotes of the command, as the book's sample does. The option also depends on the system providing a command interpreter, since the source compiles the function only if the system has one, and on the script being executable by the user who runs the node [BC]. Finally, the command runs only when a warning is newly set, because the source calls it only if the warning was not already present, so a script that sends e-mail should not be the only way to learn that the node is in trouble."),
 ]),
]

