NODES += [
 ("Data", None, 1, None, None, [
  (W, "The data branch is about what a node holds and what the interface shows of it: the transactions that move value, the blocks that gather them into the shared record, and the state of the chain that results. The section of the book on exploring transactions and blocks takes one payment, Alice's purchase from Bob's store, and follows it from a serialized hexadecimal string to a decoded structure, and then goes up a level to a block, which can be referenced either by its height or by its hash [AH]. The chapter treats them as things to look at, and this branch explains what the fields mean."),
  (Y, "A student who understands the fields can check the node's answers, which is the point of running a node. The book says that a transaction ID is not authoritative before confirmation, that a block's confirmations tell its depth, and that the median time of the block is a time measurement harder for miners to manipulate [AH]. Each of these statements is a claim about how the system is built, and each can be tested on data that anybody can fetch. The branch therefore pairs each field with a computation that reproduces it from the raw bytes."),
  (E, "The data are met whenever one asks the node about something that happened on the chain. The evidence of this version contains the real hexadecimal of Alice's transaction, the answer that release 31.1 gives when asked to decode it, the header and the list of transaction identifiers of block 123456, the times of the eleven blocks around it and the outputs of all 1,966 transactions of block 775072, taken from a public explorer, which is a third party's index and not the reader's own node [BS] [BC]."),
  (H, "The branch has three groups. The transaction group covers the serialized form, the decoded form, the witness, the identifiers and the weight. The block group covers the height and hash, the header, the weight, the Merkle root, the subsidy, the target and difficulty, and the median time. The chain state group covers the relations between blocks: the chain with the most work, the amount of work, the confirmations and the reorganization. The computations use only the Python standard library, so that every number printed in the text can be repeated by the reader."),
  (K, "Two cautions apply to the whole branch. The first is that the data in the book belong to a moment: the confirmations of a block grow with every new block, and the block of the later example is so old that its figures are history only. The second is that the book contains one printed result that does not match its script. The program that adds the outputs of block 775072 prints 10,322.07722534 BTC and explains it as including a reward of 25 BTC, but the reward at that height is 6.25 BTC, and the sum of all outputs of that block is 5,863.00566521 BTC, as the executed checks of this chapter show [AH] [BC]."),
 ], None),
 ("Transaction", None, 2, "Data", None, [
  (W, "The transaction group collects the concepts about the objects that carry value. Chapter 2 introduced the transaction with its inputs, outputs and fee; this group looks at the same object as a node sees it, first as a string of bytes, then as a decoded structure with fields, with an identifier and with a cost in block space. The book's example is Alice's payment to Bob, retrieved by its identifier and decoded, in which the transaction used one input and generated two outputs, the payment to Bob and the change back to Alice [AH]."),
  (Y, "How a transaction is stored decides how it is identified and how much it costs. A change in the serialization introduced the witness, a part of the transaction that is not included in the old identifier, and with it a second identifier and a new measure of size. A reader who knows only the simple picture of inputs and outputs will not be able to explain why Alice's transaction has a size of 194 bytes and a virtual size of 143, or why it has two identifiers. This group explains why."),
  (E, "The group's concepts are met in the output of the two commands of the book: the first returns the serialized transaction in hexadecimal notation, and the second decodes it into the fields shown in the chapter [AH]. They are met again in every block explorer and every wallet, which show the same fields under other names. The evidence of this version decodes the book's hexadecimal with both the real release 31.1 and a short Python program, and the two agree on every field [BC]."),
  (H, "The concepts follow the path from bytes to meaning. Serialization explains the layout of the bytes, decoding the structure that results, the witness the part that the old layout did not have, the identifiers the two hashes that name a transaction, the weight the way the cost is counted, and malleability the reason why an identifier cannot be relied on before the transaction is confirmed."),
 ], None),
 ("TransactionSerialization", "Transaction serialization", 3, "Transaction", ("the hexadecimal string of Alice's transaction", "Serialization writes a transaction as a sequence of bytes, with a marker and a flag before the inputs when the transaction has witness data, shown in hexadecimal notation.", (_prog(_TXP + "result = (version, marker, flag, n_in, n_out, locktime)"), "(1, 0, 1, 1, 2, 0)")), [
  (W, "Serialization is the writing of a structured object as a sequence of bytes, so that it can be stored or sent. The book shows the result for Alice's transaction as 388 hexadecimal digits, which the command getrawtransaction returns when it is asked for the transaction by its identifier [AH]. The same bytes travel between nodes, are stored in the block files and are hashed to produce identifiers. Hexadecimal notation, which chapter 2 explained, writes each byte as two digits."),
  (Y, "A precise byte layout is what lets independent programs agree. The specification BIP 144 defines the layout with witness data: a four-byte version, a marker byte that must be zero, a flag byte that must be nonzero, the number of inputs, the inputs, the number of outputs, the outputs, the witnesses and a four-byte lock time [B144]. The marker is zero so that the layout is never mistaken for a valid transaction by a program that does not know about witnesses, because in the old layout a zero would mean a transaction with no inputs [B144]."),
  (E, "A reader can take the hexadecimal in the book and read it by eye. It starts with 01000000, the version 1 written with the least significant byte first, then 00 and 01, the marker and the flag, then 01 for one input, then the 32 bytes of the previous transaction identifier written in reverse order, the four bytes of the index 1, a zero for an empty signature script and ffffffff for the sequence. Then come 02 for two outputs, each with eight bytes of value and a script, and finally the witness and the four zero bytes of the lock time [AH]."),
  (H, "The worked example is a parser of about twenty lines that follows this layout. It reads the integers with the least significant byte first, uses a count of one byte for the numbers of inputs, outputs and witness items, and returns the version 1, the marker 0, the flag 1, one input, two outputs and the lock time 0. The values of the two outputs come out as 20000 and 75000 satoshis, and the single witness item has 65 bytes, as the other checks of this branch use. The count that precedes a list is a compact number of one byte when it is less than 253, and uses more bytes after that."),
  (K, "The layout has a short and a long form. A transaction with no witness is written without the marker and the flag, and that is the form that is hashed to get the old identifier, so one transaction has two serializations. A parser that assumes one of them will misread the other, which is the reason why the call that decodes a raw transaction has an option to tell whether the hexadecimal is in the witness form, and uses a heuristic when it is not told [BC]. The numbers are also written the unexpected way round: byte order in identifiers is reversed between the display and the bytes that are hashed."),
 ]),
 ("DecodedTransaction", "Decoded transaction", 3, "Transaction", ("decoderawtransaction on Alice's transaction", "Decoding the serialized hexadecimal of Alice's payment shows its one input, two outputs, addresses and script types, exactly as the chain holds them.", ("[(o['n'], o['value'], o['scriptPubKey']['type']) for o in %s['vout']]" % _DEC, "[(0, 0.0002, 'witness_v1_taproot'), (1, 0.00075, 'witness_v0_keyhash')]")), [
  (W, "A decoded transaction is the structure that results from reading the bytes of a serialized transaction and naming each field. The command decoderawtransaction takes the hexadecimal string and returns a JSON object [AH]. The help text of the current release describes it as returning a JSON object representing the serialized, hex-encoded transaction [BC]. The object has the identifiers, the sizes, the version and the lock time, a list of inputs under the key vin and a list of outputs under the key vout."),
  (Y, "Decoding turns an opaque string into something a person can check. The book remarks that the decode shows all the components of the transaction, and that following the identifier of the input to the previous transaction lets a reader walk back through the chain of transactions as the coins pass from one owner to the next [AH]. Anyone who distrusts a wallet or an explorer can run the same decoding on their own node, which is the independent check that the chapter advocates."),
  (E, "In Alice's transaction the input refers to output number 1 of the earlier transaction 4ac5...3aeb, has an empty signature script, one witness item and the sequence number 4294967295, which is ffffffff; the lock time is 0 and the version 1 [AH] [BC]. The first output has the value 0.0002 BTC, 20000 satoshis, and a script of the type witness_v1_taproot; the second has 0.00075 BTC, 75000 satoshis, and a script of the type witness_v0_keyhash. The two outputs correspond to the payment to Bob and the change back to Alice [AH]."),
  (H, "The decoding code reads the bytes as the serialization concept describes and converts what it reads: the satoshi values become amounts in bitcoin by dividing by 100,000,000, the scripts are shown as assembly and as hexadecimal, the type of each script is recognized from its pattern, and for a known type an address is derived and shown. Each output script also has a descriptor, the field desc, whose checksum the output descriptor concept explains [BC]. The worked example takes the number, the amount and the type of each output from the answer of release 31.1."),
  (K, "The decoder shows what the bytes say, not whether they are valid or confirmed. A transaction may be well formed and still spend an output that does not exist, or one that is already spent. The values are given as decimal fractions of a bitcoin, with eight decimals, which a floating-point program should not use for money arithmetic. And the book's example gives the address of an output only because the node was able to derive it from the script: an output whose script has no standard form has no address. The call that fetches a transaction by identifier only works for mempool transactions unless the transaction index is on, or the block is named [BC]."),
 ]),
 ("SegregatedWitness", "Segregated witness", 3, "Transaction", ("the 65-byte witness of Alice's input", "Segregated witness moves the signature data of a transaction out of the part that the identifier covers, into a witness that counts less toward the size limit.", ("(194 - 125, 194 - 125 == 2 + 1 + 1 + 65)", "(69, True)")), [
  (W, "Segregated witness, often written SegWit, is an upgrade that separates the data that prove a spending right, the signatures, from the rest of the transaction. The book's glossary describes it as a proposed upgrade that separates signature data from bitcoin transactions, and as a soft fork, a change that makes the protocol rules more restrictive [AH]. In a transaction the separated data form the witness, a list of items for each input, which BIP 144 writes after the outputs and before the lock time [B144]."),
  (Y, "The upgrade solved two problems. It removed the witness from the data that the transaction identifier covers, so that a third party can no longer change an identifier by changing a signature, a point that the malleability concept develops. And it changed the way block space is measured: BIP 141 defines the weight of a block as the base size times three plus the total size, and limits it to 4,000,000, so witness bytes count one unit each while all the other bytes count four [B141]. Witness data are therefore cheaper than other data."),
  (E, "Alice's transaction uses it. Its input has an empty signature script, and all of its proof is in the witness: one item of 65 bytes, a signature of 64 bytes followed by one byte for the way it was signed [AH]. The decoded form shows the item under the key txinwitness. Of the 194 bytes of the transaction, 125 are the old layout and 69 are the marker, the flag, the number of items, the length of the item and the item itself. The output scripts show the two kinds that the upgrade created: a witness script of version 0 that pays to a key hash, and one of version 1, the taproot type."),
  (H, "Software that knows the upgrade finds a witness program in an output script: a version number followed by a short piece of data. For version 0 the program is either 20 bytes, a key hash, or 32 bytes, a script hash; versions 1 to 16 are reserved for future extensions [B141]. The block commits to the witnesses of its transactions through a second tree built from the witness identifiers, and the witness identifier of the coinbase transaction is taken to be all zeros [B141]. The worked example shows the byte accounting: 194 minus 125 is 69, which is the marker and flag, the count and the length, and the 65 bytes."),
  (K, "The upgrade was designed to be a soft fork: old nodes see an output script with a version and a program and consider anyone able to spend it, while upgraded nodes enforce the new rules, so old nodes keep accepting the chain without understanding the witness [B141]. This also means that a node which does not understand witnesses cannot verify the signatures of such transactions, even though it accepts them, which is a reason to run current software. The upgrade was activated at height 481824 on the main network, a constant that Bitcoin Core's chain parameters record [BC]."),
 ]),
 ("TransactionWeight", "Transaction weight", 3, "Transaction", ("569 weight units, 143 virtual bytes", "A transaction's weight counts its non-witness bytes four times and its witness bytes once, and its virtual size is the weight divided by four, rounded up.", ("-(-(4 * 125 + 194 - 125) // 4)", "143")), [
  (W, "The weight of a transaction is a measure of the block space it uses, in weight units. BIP 141 defines it as the base transaction size times three plus the total transaction size, and defines the virtual size as the weight divided by four, rounded up to the next integer [B141]. The base size is the size in bytes of the old serialization, without any witness data, and the total size is the size of the new one, with the witness. Equivalently, every byte outside the witness counts four units and every byte inside it counts one."),
  (Y, "Weight is the unit in which the limit of a block is set. The source of the consensus rules gives the maximum block weight as 4,000,000 and the witness scale factor as 4, so a block of only old-style bytes can contain 1,000,000 of them, and a block with witnesses can contain more [BC]. Weight is also the basis of the fee rate that chapter 2 introduced: a fee rate in satoshis per virtual byte divides the fee by the weight over four, which is why the decoded answer shows both the size and the virtual size."),
  (E, "The decoded form of Alice's transaction shows size 194, vsize 143 and weight 569, the three numbers printed in the book and reproduced by release 31.1 [AH] [BC]. The Python parser of this branch finds that the old layout is 125 bytes and the full one 194. The weight is then 3 times 125 plus 194, which is 569, and the virtual size is 569 divided by 4, which is 142.25, rounded up to 143. The worked example is the same arithmetic, written as four times the base size plus the witness bytes."),
  (H, "Weight units make the witness discount a precise rule. The 69 extra bytes of Alice's transaction, the marker, the flag and the witness, cost 69 units, whereas the 125 base bytes cost 500, so the witness is 35.6 percent of the bytes of the transaction but only 12.1 percent of its weight, which is 69 out of 569. The same transaction written without its witness would weigh 500. The rounding up is why the virtual size is a whole number, and why two transactions can have the same virtual size and different weights."),
  (K, "Virtual bytes are not bytes, and the discount does not reduce the data that nodes must store or send: a node still downloads and stores all 194 bytes. The limit on weight is a consensus rule, whereas the policy of which transactions a node relays has other limits, one of which is the minimum fee rate, so a transaction of valid weight may still not be relayed. A last point is that older material speaks of a block limit of one megabyte, a rule that the upgrade replaced by the limit of weight, and the figure quoted in the book for the block of the later example, 4179 bytes, is far below either."),
 ]),
 ("TransactionIdentifier", "Transaction identifier (txid and wtxid)", 3, "Transaction", ("txid and hash", "The txid hashes the transaction without its witness and the hash includes it; a txid is not authoritative before confirmation because a transaction can be modified.", (_prog(_TXP + "result = (txid[:16], wtxid[:16])"), "('466200308696215b', 'f7cdbc7cf8b910d3')")), [
  (W, "A transaction identifier is the name of a transaction: the double SHA-256 hash of its serialization, written with the bytes in reverse order. The transaction has two of them. BIP 141 leaves the definition of the txid unchanged, the double SHA-256 hash of the traditional serialization format, and defines a new witness identifier, the wtxid, as the double SHA-256 hash of the serialization with witness data [B141]. In the decoded answer the first appears under txid and the second under hash."),
  (Y, "Identifiers are how everything refers to a transaction: the inputs of later transactions, the lists in blocks, the Merkle tree, the requests to the node. Having a second identifier that covers the witness allows the network to commit to the signatures in the block without changing what the old identifier covers. If no input uses a witness, the two identifiers are equal [B141]. The book's tip adds the warning that a txid is not authoritative, a point that the malleability concept develops [AH]."),
  (E, "The book gives the identifier of Alice's transaction as 466200308696215bbc949d5141a49a4138ecdfdfaa2a8029c1f9bcecd1f96177 and the node's decode shows the second one under the key hash as f7cdbc7cf8b910d35cc69962e791138624e4eae7901010a6da4c02e7d238cdac [AH] [BC]. The Python parser of this branch computes both from the hexadecimal: it hashes the 125 bytes of the old layout twice with SHA-256 for the txid, and the 194 bytes of the full layout for the wtxid, and reverses the byte order of each result."),
  (H, "The reversal is the reason why identifiers, which are numbers, are written backwards: the 32-byte hash result is shown with its last byte first, the way the block hashes, which start with many zeros, are conventionally displayed. The Merkle tree of a block is built from txids, and BIP 141 adds a second tree built from wtxids, whose root is committed to in the coinbase transaction [B141]. The coinbase transaction's wtxid is assumed to be 32 zero bytes [B141]."),
  (K, "A txid can be looked up in the block files only with the transaction index or with the name of the block, as the earlier concepts explain, and the book warns that the absence of a txid from the blockchain does not mean the transaction was not processed [AH]. The wtxid is the more specific name, and is the one used when transactions are announced to peers in current protocols. It is also not secret, and not an address: two parties who know the identifier know only which transaction is meant."),
 ]),
 ("TransactionMalleability", "Transaction malleability", 3, "Transaction", ("changing a signature changes the wtxid but not the txid", "Malleability is the possibility that a transaction is modified before confirmation so that its identifier changes while its meaning does not; the witness separation removed it for the txid of witness spends.", (_prog(_TXP + "ws = bytearray(raw)\nws[-10] ^= 1\nws = bytes(ws)\nresult = (d(ws)[::-1].hex() == wtxid, d(raw[:4] + raw[6:out_end] + ws[wit_end:])[::-1].hex() == txid)"), "(False, True)")), [
  (W, "Transaction malleability is the possibility that a transaction can be changed by a third party before it is confirmed, so that its identifier changes while what it does stays the same. The book puts it in a tip: a transaction ID is not authoritative, the absence of a txid from the blockchain does not mean that the transaction was not processed, and this is known as transaction malleability because transactions can be modified before confirmation in a block, changing their txids [AH]. The reason is that the identifier hashes data, such as signatures, that someone other than the sender may be able to alter."),
  (Y, "A program that watches for a payment by its txid can be misled. If a transaction is changed in transit and the changed version is the one that is confirmed, the original identifier never appears in a block, and a program that waits for it would conclude that the payment failed although the money moved. The book's advice is to treat the txid as authoritative only after confirmation: after the transaction is included in a block, its txid cannot change unless a reorganization removes that block from the best chain, and reorganizations are rare after several confirmations [AH]."),
  (E, "The problem was met by exchanges and by protocols that build a chain of unconfirmed transactions, since the child names the parent by its txid and breaks if the parent's txid changes. Segregated witness addresses it for the transactions that use it: because the txid does not cover the witness, a change of the signature changes the wtxid but not the txid [B141]. The worked example shows this on Alice's transaction: it flips one bit of the signature, which makes the transaction invalid but serves to show the identifiers. The wtxid changes, the txid does not, and all the bytes before the witness are unchanged."),
  (H, "The old identifier covers the version, the inputs, the outputs and the lock time. For inputs that spend a witness output the signature script is empty and the signature is in the witness, outside that hash; so no one can change what the txid covers without changing the transaction's meaning, since the signatures commit to those fields. For old-style inputs the signature script is covered, and the signature encoding could be altered, which is why the BIPs of the time tightened what is accepted. The wtxid covers everything and so does change."),
  (K, "Segregated witness does not make every transaction immune. A transaction that spends an old-style output still has its signature script in the txid, and a transaction can still be replaced by a conflicting one that spends the same inputs, which is a different matter from malleability, since it has different outputs or a different meaning. The wtxid, in turn, can still change with the witness. And the TIP of the book is still true for the careful programmer: use the transaction's confirmation, not only its identifier, to know that a payment was made."),
 ]),
 ("Block", None, 2, "Data", None, [
  (W, "The block group collects the concepts about the unit in which transactions are recorded. A block holds a header, which is a small fixed-size summary, and a list of transactions, which makes up most of its size; the book of the chapter on the blockchain says that the header is 80 bytes while the transactions of a block can total much more [AH]. In the chapter on Bitcoin Core the block is explored with two commands: one finds a block's hash from its height, and the other returns the block as a JSON object."),
  (Y, "Knowing the block's fields is knowing how the chain is held together. Each block names its parent by the hash of its header, so changing anything in a block changes its hash and breaks the link to the next. The header also commits to the transactions through the Merkle root, to the work that was spent through the target and the nonce, and to the time through the timestamp. The book says of the fields of the answer that some are used for security and proof of work: merkle root, nonce, bits, difficulty and chainwork [AH]."),
  (E, "The reader meets the group in the answer of the block command for block 123456 in the book: its hash, 13 transactions, a height, the number of confirmations, the version, the Merkle root, the time, the median time, the nonce, the bits, the difficulty, the chain work, the sizes and the list of transaction identifiers [AH]. The evidence of this version confirms the hash of this block from its header, its Merkle root from its 13 identifiers, and its difficulty from its bits, using the public data of an explorer [BS]."),
  (H, "The concepts of the group go from the outside to the inside. The height and the hash name a block, the header holds what is hashed, the weight measures the size, the Merkle root summarizes the transactions, the subsidy is the new money it creates, the compact target and the difficulty say how hard it was to make, and the median time is the clock rule that bounds its timestamp."),
 ], None),
 ("BlockHeight", "Block height", 3, "Block", ("getblockhash 123456", "A block is found by its height, how many blocks precede it, or by its header hash; its confirmations count the blocks built on top, itself included.", ("123456 + 651742 - 1", "775197")), [
  (W, "The height of a block is its position in the chain: the number of blocks that precede it. The book says that the height tells how many blocks preceded the block, and the glossary of the chapter on the blockchain adds that the first block, the genesis block, is at height 0 [AH]. The description in the source code is the same: the genesis block has height 0, and the field of the chain information called blocks gives the height of the chain of most work that has been fully validated [BC]."),
  (Y, "Heights give people a convenient name for a block, because they are short and ordered. A hash identifies a block without doubt, but it cannot be read or ordered; a height can be said aloud and compared. The book says that blocks can be referenced either by their height or by their hash, and the first command of the exploration, getblockhash, takes a height and returns the block header hash [AH]. The consensus rules also use heights: they decide when the reward is halved and when an upgrade becomes active."),
  (E, "In the book the command getblockhash 123456 returns 0000000000002917ed80650c6174aac8dfc46f5fe36480aaef682ff6cd83c3ca [AH]. For block 1000 it returns 00000000c937983704a73af28acdec37b049d214adbda81d7e2a3dd146f6ed09, which the evidence of this version checks by hashing the block's header [BS]. In the source of Bitcoin Core the heights of the rule changes are constants of the main network, for example 227931 for the rule of BIP 34, 481824 for segregated witness, and 210000 as the interval between halvings of the subsidy [BC]."),
  (H, "The height is not stored in the header and is not part of a block's identity: it is the length of the path to the genesis block, and the node counts it in its index. This also means that two blocks may have the same height, if there is a fork, though only one of them can be part of the best chain. The relation to depth is simple: the depth of a block is the height of the tip minus its height plus one. For block 123456, whose book printout says 651742 confirmations, the tip then has height 123456 + 651742 - 1 = 775197, as the worked example computes."),
  (K, "Height 0 is the first block and not the second, so the number of blocks in a chain is its height plus one. A height is also not a proof: the same height names different blocks on different chains, and BIP 34 made the height a part of the coinbase of each block precisely so that it is unambiguous [B34]. The height of the tip changes every ten minutes or so, and any figure quoted in the book, such as the assumption that the node is at least at block 775,072, is a snapshot of that day [AH]."),
 ]),
 ("BlockHash", "Block hash", 3, "Block", ("the 80-byte header hashed twice", "The block hash is the double SHA-256 hash of the 80-byte block header, written backwards; it identifies a block uniquely and is not stored in the block.", (_prog(_HEADER + "\nresult = result[0]"), "True")), [
  (W, "The block hash is the identifier of a block. The book on the blockchain explains that the primary identifier of a block is its cryptographic hash, a commitment made by hashing the block header twice through the SHA-256 algorithm, and that the resulting 32-byte hash is called the block hash but is more accurately the block header hash, because only the header is used to compute it [AH]. The first block of the chain has the hash 000000000019d6689c085ae165831e934ff763ae46a2a6c172b3f1b60a8ce26f, which the log and the chain information of the release 31.1 also show [AH] [BC]."),
  (Y, "A single hash stands for the entire block, because the header contains the Merkle root of the transactions, and the next block's header contains this hash as the previous block hash. Anyone who knows the hash of the latest block can in principle check the whole chain behind it. The book says that the hash identifies a block uniquely and unambiguously and can be independently derived by any node by hashing the header [AH]."),
  (E, "The hashes in the chapter all begin with a long string of zeros: 0000000000002917... for block 123456 and 00000000c9379837... for block 1000. These zeros are the proof of work that the miners did, as chapter 2 explained: the hash of a header had to be below a target [AH]. The worked example takes the header of block 123456 from the explorer's data, with the version, the previous hash, the Merkle root, the time, the bits and the nonce, hashes it, and compares the result with the printed hash; for block 1000 the check in the next concept does the same."),
  (H, "The header's fields are packed in a fixed order into 80 bytes: the version as four bytes with the least significant first, the 32 bytes of the previous block hash and 32 bytes of the Merkle root, both reversed from their display order, and the time, the bits and the nonce as four bytes each. The packed bytes are put through SHA-256 twice, and the 32-byte result is reversed to produce the familiar display. The book notes that the hash is not included in the block's data structure; each node computes it as the block arrives and may store it in a separate index [AH]."),
  (K, "A hash is not an authority. It tells which block is meant, not whether the block is valid or part of the best chain; a block with a very small hash can still be invalid because of a transaction in it. The display order differs from the order used in computation, and a program that forgets to reverse the bytes gets a different value. And the explorer's data used here are a secondary source: the check confirms that the explorer's fields are consistent with each other, which is what the hash promises, not that the explorer is honest about the chain."),
 ]),
 ("BlockHeader", "Block header", 3, "Block", ("six fields in 80 bytes", "The block header holds the version, the previous block hash, the Merkle root, the timestamp, the compact target and the nonce, in 80 bytes.", ("4 + 32 + 32 + 4 + 4 + 4", "80")), [
  (W, "The block header is the summary at the head of every block. The book's table lists its six fields and their sizes: the version, four bytes; the previous block hash, 32 bytes, a hash of the previous block in the chain; the Merkle root, 32 bytes, the root of the Merkle tree of the block's transactions; the timestamp, four bytes, the approximate creation time as Unix epoch time; the target, four bytes, a compact encoding of the proof-of-work target; and the nonce, four bytes, arbitrary data used for the proof-of-work algorithm [AH]. The sum, 4 + 32 + 32 + 4 + 4 + 4, is 80 bytes."),
  (Y, "The header is small on purpose. Because it commits to everything else, a program that holds only the headers can follow the chain and its work without the transactions. This is what the node in the book does when it starts: it first fetches the block headers from its peers to find the blockchain with the most proof of work, and afterward continues to download the full blocks [AH]. Nakamoto's paper made the same point about lighter programs: a user needs to keep only a copy of the block headers of the longest proof-of-work chain [NK]."),
  (E, "The fields are found under the same names in the answer of the block command: version, merkleroot, time, nonce, bits and previousblockhash, together with the hash itself [AH]. For block 123456 the version is 1, the time 1305200806 and the nonce 2436437219; its header is the 80-byte string whose double hash is the block hash [BS]. The chain information of the node shows bits and target for the best block as well [BC]."),
  (H, "Each field has a role in the chain. The previous block hash links the block to its parent. The Merkle root ties the transactions to the header. The target says how small the hash has to be, and the nonce is what the miner changes to try again, together with the timestamp and, indirectly, the contents of the coinbase transaction. The timestamp is a Unix time, a count of seconds since the first of January 1970, and it must obey the rules of the median time and of the two-hour limit [AH]."),
  (K, "The version field no longer means a version. The book's table says it was originally a version field and its use has evolved; the upgrade mechanism of BIP 9 uses its bits to signal readiness, as the soft fork concept explains [AH] [B9]. The header's time is set by the miner and is only roughly right. And the header does not contain the number of transactions, the block's height or its hash, which are all derived."),
 ]),
 ("BlockWeight", "Block weight", 3, "Block", ("weight 16716 for size 4179", "A block without witness data weighs four times its size in weight units, which the getblock result shows as size, strippedsize and weight.", ("4 * 4179", "16716")), [
  (W, "The weight of a block is its size in weight units, calculated like the weight of a transaction: BIP 141 defines it as the base size times three plus the total size, where the base size is the size without witness data and the total size is the size with it [B141]. The block command returns three measurements, which the book describes as the legacy stripped size, the full size, and the size in weight units [AH]. The rule is that the block weight must not exceed 4,000,000 [B141]."),
  (Y, "The limit decides how many transactions a block can hold, and so how much the network can do in ten minutes. It replaced the earlier limit of 1,000,000 bytes of total size with a combined measure, so that witness data, which is cheaper to verify and does not burden the set of unspent outputs, is charged less [B141]. The limit is also a constant in the source: the maximum block weight is 4,000,000, and the witness scale factor is 4 [BC]."),
  (E, "For block 123456, which was mined years before the upgrade, the book's answer shows a stripped size of 4179, a size of 4179 and a weight of 16716 [AH]. When a block has no witness data, the base size and the total size are the same, so the weight is 3 times 4179 plus 4179, or simply 4 times 4179, which is 16716, as the worked example computes. The explorer's data for the block agree: 13 transactions, size 4179 and weight 16716 [BS]."),
  (H, "The node computes the weight by serializing the block twice, once without and once with witness data, and combining the sizes; the source shows the three results being filled into the answer as strippedsize, size and weight [BC]. A block with witness data has a total size larger than its stripped size, and a weight between four times the stripped size and the total size times 4, so a full block of heavy-witness transactions can have a total size well above one million bytes while staying below the limit in weight units."),
  (K, "Weight is not the same as size and the two should not be mixed. A tool that reports a block size in bytes and one that reports it in virtual bytes give different numbers for the same block, and a reader who compares them with the limit of 4,000,000 must use the weight. The limit is also a rule for miners, not for relay nodes, which means that a block over the limit is invalid, whereas a transaction is only rejected by policy before that."),
 ]),
 ("MerkleRoot", "Merkle root", 3, "Block", ("the Merkle root of the block's transactions", "The Merkle root in a block header commits to every transaction of the block, and hashing the block's transaction identifiers pairwise up to one value reproduces it.", (_prog(_MERKLE), "'0e60651a9934e8f0decd1c5fde39309e48fca0cd1c84a21ddfde95033762d86c'")), [
  (W, "The Merkle root is the single hash in a block header that stands for all the transactions of the block. The glossary of the book describes a Merkle tree as a tree constructed by hashing paired data, the leaves, then pairing and hashing the results until a single hash remains, the Merkle root; in Bitcoin the leaves are almost always the transactions of one block [AH]. The root is a descendant of all the hashed pairs in the tree, and a block header must include a valid root for the transactions in the block [AH]."),
  (Y, "The root makes the header a commitment to the contents. Changing, adding, removing or reordering any transaction changes the root, which changes the header and so the block hash, and the proof of work would have to be redone. It also allows a short proof of membership: to show that one transaction is in a block, one needs only the hashes along the path from the transaction to the root, not the whole block, a property that Nakamoto's paper describes when it explains how payments can be verified without running a full node [NK]."),
  (E, "The answer of the block command shows the root under merkleroot and lists the transaction identifiers under tx; for block 123456 it lists 13 [AH]. The evidence of this version takes these 13 identifiers from the explorer, builds the tree and obtains 0e60651a9934e8f0decd1c5fde39309e48fca0cd1c84a21ddfde95033762d86c, which is the Merkle root in the header of the block and the one printed in the book [BS] [AH]. In the header it occupies 32 bytes."),
  (H, "The tree is built from the bottom. The identifiers of the transactions are the leaves, taken in their bytes in the internal order, which is the reverse of the display order; each pair is joined and hashed twice with SHA-256, giving a level with half as many nodes; and the process repeats until one hash remains. When a level has an odd number of nodes, the last one is paired with itself. With 13 leaves the levels have 13, 7, 4, 2 and 1 nodes, because the lists of 13 and 7 are each extended by a copy of the last element before pairing, as the worked example does."),
  (K, "The rule of duplicating the last element has a known weakness: two different lists of transactions can give the same root if the later one repeats the last transaction, and for this reason a node must check a block's transactions for duplication in addition to the root. The root is also computed from txids, not wtxids, so it does not commit to the witnesses; the second tree of BIP 141 does [B141]. And a root is not evidence of validity: it proves only that the listed transactions are those that the miner hashed."),
 ]),
 ("BlockSubsidy", "Block subsidy", 3, "Block", ("6.25 bitcoins at height 775072", "The subsidy starts at 50 bitcoins and is shifted right once for every 210,000 blocks, so it is 6.25 bitcoins at height 775072.", ("50 * 10**8 >> (775072 // 210000)", "625000000")), [
  (W, "The block subsidy is the amount of new bitcoin that a block creates, which the miner may claim in the first transaction of the block, the coinbase transaction. Bitcoin Core computes it in a short function: the number of halvings is the height divided by the halving interval of 210,000 blocks, the subsidy starts at 50 bitcoins, and it is shifted right once for each halving, which divides it by two each time [BC]. Chapter 2 introduced the block reward, which is this subsidy plus the fees of the block's transactions."),
  (Y, "The schedule is what makes the supply of bitcoin finite and predictable. The amounts are counted in satoshis, 100,000,000 to a bitcoin, so each shift right halves an integer; the sum of all the subsidies over the 64 possible periods is 2,099,999,997,690,000 satoshis, which is slightly below 21 million bitcoins by 2,310,000 satoshis, because of the rounding in the shifts, as the worked checks of this branch compute [BC]. The consensus constant for the maximum amount of money is 21,000,000 times the number of satoshis in a bitcoin [BC]."),
  (E, "The book's program that adds the outputs of block 775072 says that the total includes a reward of 25 bitcoins [AH]. The subsidy at that height is not 25: 775072 divided by 210000 is 3 with a remainder, so there have been three halvings and the subsidy is 50 shifted right three times, which is 6.25 bitcoins, or 625000000 satoshis, as the worked example computes. The coinbase transaction of that block in the explorer's data pays out 633799108 satoshis, which is 625000000 plus fees of 8799108, and the sum of the fees of the other 1,965 transactions is exactly that number [BS]."),
  (H, "The coinbase transaction cannot create more than the subsidy plus the fees of the block, and the amount is checked by every node. The function ends in zero for a height of 64 halvings or more, because a shift by 64 or more is undefined in the language of the program and the code returns zero instead [BC]. A coinbase output also cannot be spent until it is 100 blocks deep, a constant named the coinbase maturity in the consensus source [BC]. For block 775072 the numbers fit: 633,799,108 less 625,000,000 is 8,799,108."),
  (K, "The book's text mentions a reward of 25 BTC and 0.0909 BTC in fees for the block of its example, and the total of 10,322.07722534 BTC. Neither matches block 775072: its reward is 6.25 BTC, its fees are 0.08799108 BTC, and the sum of all its outputs is 5,863.00566521 BTC, so the printed result does not belong to that block, and a student who compares with an explorer, as the book suggests, will find the difference [AH] [BS]. 25 BTC was the reward for blocks 420,000 to 629,999. The error shows why printed numbers should be checked."),
 ]),
 ("CompactTarget", "Compact target (bits)", 3, "Block", ("bits 1a6a93b3", "The bits field is a four-byte compact form of the target: the first byte is the length in bytes and the next three are the leading digits, so the target is the digits shifted left by the length minus three bytes.", ("'%064x' % (0x6a93b3 << (8 * (0x1a - 3)))", "'0000000000006a93b30000000000000000000000000000000000000000000000'")), [
  (W, "The compact target is the way in which the header records the proof-of-work target in only four bytes. The target is a 256-bit number that the hash of a valid block must not exceed, as chapter 2 explained for the difficulty target, and a 256-bit number would need 32 bytes. The header's field, called bits, holds instead an exponent in the first byte and a mantissa in the other three. The book's table calls the field a compact encoding of the proof-of-work target [AH]."),
  (Y, "Four bytes were enough because the target needs only a few digits of precision: it changes in small steps, and the lower digits would not matter. A compact form keeps the header at 80 bytes. The chain information of the current release shows both the compact form, in the field bits, and the target in full, which the book's printout of 2023 does not have [BC]. The form also has a sign bit, a legacy of the number type it was borrowed from, which Bitcoin Core rejects as a target when it is set."),
  (E, "The block printed in the book has the bits 1a6a93b3, which is 443192243 in decimal and the value the explorer gives for block 123456 [AH] [BS]. The first byte, 0x1a, is 26, and the other three bytes are 0x6a93b3. For the first block the value is 1d00ffff, and the chain information of the evidence node shows it together with the full target, 00000000ffff followed by zeros [BC]."),
  (H, "The source decodes the field with two lines: the size is the value shifted right by 24 bits, the word is the value masked by 0x007fffff, and the target is the word shifted left by 8 times the size minus 3 bits, that is, by size minus three bytes [BC]. For 0x1a6a93b3 this puts the three bytes 6a 93 b3 followed by 23 zero bytes, which written in 64 hexadecimal digits is 0000000000006a93b300...00, with 12 leading zero digits, as the worked example prints. The reverse conversion rounds a target down to three significant bytes, so not every number is the target of a block."),
  (K, "Because only three bytes of the target are kept, the true target that the hash is compared with is the decoded one, not the one the retargeting formula first computed, and a table of difficulties from a program that uses the full-precision number can differ by a tiny amount from one that uses bits. A second caution is the sign bit: the code reduces the mantissa and raises the size if the top bit of the three bytes is set, so values such as 0x1d00ffff and not 0x1d0100.. are seen in practice. And the header's field is named bits in the answer but target in the book's table [AH] [BC]."),
 ]),
 ("Difficulty", "Difficulty", 3, "Block", ("difficulty from bits 1a6a93b3", "A block's difficulty follows from its bits, the compact form of its target, as the ratio of the easiest target to this block's target.", ("round(0xffff * 256**(0x1d - 3) / (0x6a93b3 * 256**(0x1a - 3)), 10)", "157416.4018436489")), [
  (W, "The difficulty is a number that tells how much harder it is to produce a valid block than it was at the start. The glossary of the book defines it as a network-wide setting that controls how much computation is required to produce a proof of work [AH]. It is defined relative to the easiest allowed target, that of the first block, so the first block has difficulty 1, as the chain information of the evidence node shows, and a block with a target a hundred times smaller has difficulty 100."),
  (Y, "The network keeps the time between blocks near ten minutes by changing the difficulty. The consensus parameters give a target spacing of 10 minutes and a target timespan of two weeks, which is 2016 blocks; after every 2016 blocks the target is multiplied by the time the last 2016 blocks actually took and divided by two weeks, and the change in one step is limited to a factor of four in either direction [BC]. The book says that every node retargets independently, every 2,016 blocks, and notes a small bias of 0.05 percent toward higher difficulty because the calculation uses 2,015 intervals [AH]."),
  (E, "For block 123456 the answer of the block command shows the difficulty 157416.4018436489 and the bits 1a6a93b3 [AH]. The worked example computes the ratio of the two targets: 0xffff times 256 to the power 26 over 0x6a93b3 times 256 to the power 23, which is 157416.4018436489. The explorer gives the same value with one more digit, 157416.40184364893, since the book's printout is rounded to its sixteen significant digits [BS]. In the chain information of release 31.1 the first block has difficulty 1 [BC]."),
  (H, "The program in Bitcoin Core computes it from the bits with floating-point arithmetic: it takes 0x0000ffff divided by the mantissa, and multiplies or divides by 256 until the exponent is 29, the value for the easiest target [BC]. Written as an exact ratio the formula is the same as the example. The retargeting code in pow.cpp clamps the timespan to between a quarter and four times the target timespan, multiplies the old target by the timespan, divides by two weeks, and never allows a target above the proof-of-work limit [BC]."),
  (K, "Difficulty is a result and not a setting that anyone chooses: it follows the hash rate of miners with a delay of up to two weeks, so a sudden fall in mining power slows blocks until the next adjustment. It is also relative: a difficulty of 157,416 at one time and one of 53,911,173,001,054 at another, the figure printed in the book's cookie example, say how the work grew, not how much work either was [AH]. A change in a test network may follow different rules, since the parameters allow a minimum-difficulty exception there [BC]."),
 ]),
 ("MedianTimePast", "Median time past", 3, "Block", ("the median of 11 block times", "The median time past is the middle value of the timestamps of the last 11 blocks, which the consensus rules use as the clock for time limits because a single miner cannot move it.", (_MTP_INC, "1305197900")), [
  (W, "The median time past, often written MTP, is a clock that the chain defines for itself. For a block it is the median, the middle value in sorted order, of the timestamps of that block and the ten before it. Bitcoin Core's source states the number of blocks as the constant 11, sorts the times and returns the element in the middle [BC]. The book of the chapter on mining says that no node will accept a block with a time less than or equal to the median time of the last 11 blocks, called the median time past [AH]."),
  (Y, "The timestamps in headers are set by the miners, who have some latitude, and the book explains the danger: a miner could set the time in the future to lower the difficulty, or in the past to make it look as if blocks were slow, or to collect the fees of transactions with time locks that have not matured [AH]. The median of eleven blocks is hard for one miner to move; BIP 113 made it the time that the checks on time locks use, so that the incentive to lie disappears [B113]."),
  (E, "The answer of the block command shows the median under mediantime. For block 123456 it is 1305197900, while the block's own timestamp is 1305200806, which is 2906 seconds later [AH] [BS]. The chain information of the node shows the same field for the best block, and for the first block, which has no predecessors, the median equals its own time [BC]. The source applies the median of the previous block to check a new block's time, with the error time-too-old [BC]."),
  (H, "The worked example takes the times of blocks 123446 to 123456, the eleven blocks ending with block 123456, sorts them and picks the sixth, which is 1305197900, the time of block 123451. This is exactly the value the node reports. The time-lock rule works one block later: the median used when checking a new block is that of its parent, and a block whose time is not greater than it is rejected [BC] [B113]. The consensus time is thus usually about an hour behind the wall clock, as the book notes [AH]."),
  (K, "The book describes the field as the median time of the 11 blocks that precede the block, but the code includes the block itself. The eleven blocks that precede block 123456 are blocks 123445 to 123455, whose median is 1305195947, not the 1305197900 that the node prints, so the sentence of the chapter is slightly off, and the median of the chapter on mining is the one that the field shows [AH] [BC]. Timestamps are also not required to increase from block to block, only to exceed the median, so a sorted list of times can differ from the order of blocks."),
 ]),
 ("ChainState", "Chain state", 2, "Data", None, [
  (W, "The chain state group collects the concepts that describe the blocks as a whole: which chain is the true one, how much work it represents, how deep a block is in it, and what happens when the node changes its mind. The word has a second meaning in Bitcoin Core, where the chain state is also the name of the database of unspent outputs, as the earlier concept on the set of unspent outputs explained; here the word means the shape of the chain [BC]."),
  (Y, "A blockchain is not a single list but a tree, since two miners may find blocks at about the same time. The rule that resolves the tree is simple, and everything a user can trust depends on it. The book says that by selecting the greatest-cumulative-work valid chain all nodes eventually achieve network-wide consensus, and that temporary discrepancies between chains are resolved as more work is added [AH]. The concepts of this group make that rule precise."),
  (E, "The group shows in the answers of the node: the chain work in the chain information and in the block data, the confirmations in the block data and the transaction data, and the reorganization only when the best chain changes, an event that the log records. The book's tip about transaction IDs refers to it, since a txid can change only if a reorganization removes the block that holds the transaction [AH]."),
  (H, "The order is from the rule to its consequences. The best chain is the one with the most work, the chain work is the measure of that work, the confirmations measure how many blocks lie above a transaction, and a reorganization is the switch from one chain to another. Together they explain why six confirmations are considered enough, as the glossary of the book states, and why a transaction in a recent block is not final [AH]."),
 ], None),
 ("BestChain", "Best chain", 3, "ChainState", ("the chain with the most work", "The best chain is the valid chain with the most cumulative proof of work; it is not necessarily the one with the most blocks.", (_prog("a_work = 3 * (2**256 // (0xffff * 256**26 + 1))\nb_work = 2 * (2**256 // ((0xffff * 256**26) // 4 + 1))\nresult = (a_work < b_work, max([('A', a_work), ('B', b_work)], key=lambda c: c[1])[0])"), "(True, 'B')")), [
  (W, "The best chain is the chain of valid blocks that a node regards as the true record. Nakamoto's paper states the rule as the longest proof-of-work chain, the one that represents the greatest effort [NK]. The book of the chapter on mining restates it precisely as the greatest-cumulative-work valid chain [AH]. The end of the best chain is called its tip, and the node's chain information reports the height of the most-work fully validated chain as the number of blocks [BC]."),
  (Y, "Without such a rule nodes would not agree, and without agreement there would be no single record of who owns what. Because blocks are found by a random process, two miners sometimes find a block at nearly the same time, and the chain forks: the glossary of the book describes this accidental fork as the situation when two or more blocks have the same height [AH]. The rule tells every node which branch to follow, and as the next block is found on one of them, that branch gains work and the other is abandoned."),
  (E, "A node applies the rule whenever a new block arrives. The book describes it: the node looks at the block's previous block hash and tries to find that parent in the existing blockchain; most of the time the parent is the tip of the best chain, and the new block extends it; sometimes it does not, and then the node attaches the header to a secondary chain and compares the work of the two [AH]. The setting of the block header on a branch and the fields of the answer, such as confirmations equal to -1 for a block that is not on the main chain, show this to the user [BC]."),
  (H, "Work, not length, decides. The chain work concept shows how to measure it: a block with a smaller target represents more expected hashes. The worked example compares an invented chain A of three blocks at the first block's target with an invented chain B of two blocks at a target four times smaller. Chain A has three units of work and chain B has two blocks of four units each, a ratio of 8 to 3, so B has about 2.67 times the work, although it is shorter, and a node would follow B. These numbers are made up for the illustration."),
  (K, "The best chain is a local judgment that nodes make with the same rule, not an announcement. At any moment two honest nodes can briefly hold different tips, and a payment in a block at the tip is not final. The longest-chain wording of the paper is a shorthand that was precise in a time when every block had the same difficulty, and the more exact wording is the one with the most work. Also, the rule applies only to valid blocks, and a chain with more work that contains an invalid block is not the best chain."),
 ]),
 ("ChainWork", "Chain work", 3, "ChainState", ("chainwork 100010001", "The work of a block is 2^256 divided by its target plus one, the expected number of hashes needed to find it, and the chain work is the sum of the work of all blocks up to it.", ("hex(2**256 // (0xffff * 256**26 + 1))", "'0x100010001'")), [
  (W, "The chain work is the total amount of proof of work behind a block, expressed as the expected number of hashes needed to produce the chain up to it. The help text of the block command defines it as the expected number of hashes required to produce the chain up to this block, in hexadecimal [BC]. It is the sum, over all blocks from the first to this one, of the work of each block, and the work of a block is the expected number of hashes needed to find a hash below its target."),
  (Y, "This is the quantity that nodes compare to choose the best chain. The source of Bitcoin Core stores it in the index entry of each block, described as the total amount of work, the expected number of hashes, in the chain up to and including that block [BC]. Because it adds numbers that depend only on the targets in the headers, it is the same in every node, and a node can compute the work of a chain from its headers alone, before having any transactions, which is why the node of the book fetches headers first [AH]."),
  (E, "The chain information of the evidence node shows chainwork 0000...0100010001 for the first block, and the book's printout has the same ending for a node that had only the first block [AH] [BC]. The block data of block 123456 in the book shows a much longer string ending in 541788211ac227bc, abbreviated in the printout [AH]. The unit is hashes, so the value 0x100010001, which is 4295032833, means that about 4.3 billion hashes are expected to find the first block."),
  (H, "The work of one block is computed in the source as 2 to the power 256 divided by the target plus one, with a trick to avoid the number 2 to the power 256, which a 256-bit integer cannot hold: it computes the complement of the target divided by the target plus one, and adds one [BC]. For the first block's target, 0xffff times 256 to the power 26, the result is 4295032833, which is hexadecimal 100010001, exactly the chain work in the node's answer, as the worked example and the checks show. The work of a block with a target of 0x6a93b3 shifted by 23 bytes is about 16.7 trillion hashes."),
  (K, "The chain work counts expected work and not work actually done: the miner of a block may have been lucky or unlucky. It is also not a measure of the value of the chain, only of its cost to reproduce. The number is large and is shown in hexadecimal with leading zeros, which makes it easy to misread; a comparison of two such strings is a comparison of numbers with the same number of digits. And it is a quantity that the node computes itself from the headers, so it is not something that a peer tells it."),
 ]),
 ("Confirmation", "Confirmation", 3, "ChainState", ("651742 confirmations for block 123456", "A confirmation counts the blocks from a transaction's block to the tip, itself included; each one makes it harder to reverse the transaction.", ("775197 - 123456 + 1", "651742")), [
  (W, "A confirmation is a block added at or above the block that contains a transaction. The glossary of the book says that once a transaction is included in a block it has one confirmation, as soon as another block is mined on the same blockchain it has two, and so on [AH]. The block command shows the number under confirmations and the book explains that it tells the depth of the block, how many blocks have been built on top of it, indicating the difficulty of changing any of the transactions in it [AH]."),
  (Y, "Confirmations are how a user decides when a payment is safe enough. A transaction in the mempool has none and can be replaced or dropped; one in a block at the tip has one and can still be undone by a reorganization; with each block on top, undoing it needs more work. The glossary says that six or more confirmations is considered sufficient proof that a transaction cannot be reversed, and the book's tip says that reorganizations are rare after a transaction has several confirmations [AH]. How many are enough depends on the value."),
  (E, "In the book the block 123456 has 651742 confirmations, and the source shows how the node computes the number: the height of the tip minus the height of the block plus one, or -1 if the block is not on the main chain [BC]. The worked example reverses it: if the tip is at height 775197, the block at 123456 has 775197 - 123456 + 1 = 651742 confirmations, and the tip itself has one. The call for a raw transaction also reports confirmations for a transaction in a block [BC]."),
  (H, "Each new block extends the chain that already contains the transaction, so the number grows by one with every block on the best chain. If a different branch overtakes the chain, the block that held the transaction leaves the best chain and its confirmations become -1 in the block data, until the transaction is included again in a block of the new chain. An attacker who wants to reverse a transaction with k confirmations has to produce a chain with more work than the honest chain from the block before it, which is the argument that Nakamoto's paper makes with probabilities [NK]."),
  (K, "A confirmation is a count, not a guarantee; its meaning grows with the work of the blocks, and a confirmation in a low-difficulty chain is worth less. The conventional six is a convention of the book and of its era, not a rule of the protocol, and a large payment may warrant waiting for more. Another trap is that different programs count differently: some start from zero for a transaction in the tip block, while Bitcoin Core counts that block as the first confirmation [BC]."),
 ]),
 ("Reorganization", "Chain reorganization", 3, "ChainState", ("switching to a branch with more work", "A reorganization occurs when a node switches to a different chain with more work: blocks leave the best chain, their transactions return to the mempool unless the new chain includes them, and the set of unspent outputs is updated.", (_prog("a = {'tx1', 'tx2', 'tx3'}\nb = {'tx1', 'tx4'}\nresult = (sorted(a - b), sorted(b - a))"), "(['tx2', 'tx3'], ['tx4'])")), [
  (W, "A reorganization is the event in which a node changes which chain it regards as the best. The book of the chapter on mining describes it: when the new block does not extend the best chain, the node attaches its header to a secondary chain and compares the work; if the secondary chain is now the best chain, the node will reorganize its view of confirmed transactions and available UTXOs, and a miner will construct a candidate block extending the new chain [AH]. The blocks that were on the old chain and are not on the new one leave the best chain."),
  (Y, "Reorganizations are the mechanism by which the network resolves forks without any authority. They matter to a user because they are the only way in which a confirmed transaction can become unconfirmed. The book's tip says the txid of a confirmed transaction cannot change unless there is a reorganization that removes the block from the best chain, and that such reorganizations are rare after a transaction has several confirmations [AH]. Most reorganizations are only one block deep."),
  (E, "A reader meets the term in the book's discussion of identifiers and of mining, in the documentation of the node's log, which records the switch, and in wallets, which may show a payment as unconfirmed again. It is also the reason why a node keeps for each block data to undo it, the files called rev in the data directory, which Bitcoin Core's file documentation describes as block undo data [BC]. And the alert command can be triggered by a related event, when the node finds an invalid chain with more work than its own [BC]."),
  (H, "When the new chain has more work, the node disconnects the blocks of the old branch from the tip down to the point where the branches split, which restores the outputs those blocks spent and removes the ones they created in the set of unspent outputs, and then connects the blocks of the new branch. The transactions of the disconnected blocks that are not in the new blocks go back to the mempool, where they may be mined again. The worked example represents the two branches by invented sets of transactions: tx2 and tx3 lose their confirmation, tx4 gains one, and tx1 is confirmed in both, and so never changes."),
  (K, "A reorganization does not undo a payment by itself: the transaction usually returns to the pool and is confirmed again in a later block, unless it conflicts with a transaction in the new chain, for instance one that spent the same output. The dangerous case is a double spend, in which the sender pays twice and tries to make the second payment win, and this is the attack that the number of confirmations guards against. A last caution is the scale: after an attack, a deeper reorganization needs more work than the honest network, and the argument depends on no single party controlling most of it [NK]."),
 ]),
]

